etymology_text:
From Proto-Turkic *(i)aĺa- (“to eat”), derived from Proto-Turkic *(i)aĺ (“food, meal”).
Cognate with
Old Uyghur [script needed] (aša-, “to eat”);
Kumyk ашамакъ (aşamaq, “to eat”);
Karachay-Balkar ашаргъа (aşarğa, “to eat”);
Yakut аһаа (ahaa, “to eat”), etc.

From Proto-Turkic *kïyn- (“hard, difficult”).
Cognate with
Old Uyghur [script needed] (qïjïn, “torment; punishment”);
Tatar кыен (qıyın, “difficult”),
Kazakh қиын (qıyın, “difficult”);
Kyrgyz кыйын (kıyın, “difficult”),
Uzbek qiyin (“difficult”),
Uyghur قىيىن (qiyin, “difficult”);
Southern Altai кыйын (kïyïn, “torment, suffering; punishment”).

From Proto-Turkic *ört (“fire”).
Cognate with Old Uyghur [script needed] (ört, “fire”);
Kazakh өрт (ört, “fire”);
Yakut өрт (ört, “burning dry grass; wildfire”).
